<% content_for :title, "Move money" %>

<%# MOVING MONEY BETWEEN TWO ENVELOPES — spec §5's reallocation, and the single-row twin of the
    distribution screen. The subtitle is the invariant said in words: allocating money is an act of
    intention rather than of location (two-ledger spec §2), so nothing physical moves at all.

    IT SAYS THE INVARIANT WITHOUT THE INTERNAL NOUN (design review H10). "One move on the purpose
    ledger" named a structure this app has never shown a user: there is no screen, no label and no
    other sentence anywhere that mentions a purpose ledger, so the one phrase carrying the reason
    this screen is safe was the one phrase nobody could decode. The reassurance is the same and the
    words are the user's own — the money is already theirs; this only changes what it is for.

    CENTRED, LIKE EVERY OTHER FORM IN THIS APP. The category form, the rule form and the account
    form are all `mx-auto` under a full-width header; this one was left-aligned in a 1440px column,
    so its card ended a third of the way across the screen with the header spanning all of it. %>
<%= page_header(title: "Move money",
                subtitle: "Change what money you already have is set aside for — " \
                          "your bank balance doesn't change") %>

<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6">
  <%# THE MOVE WAS REFUSED. Above everything else, because the screen below it is a proposal again
      and there is nothing in the figures to say so. The second sentence is the fact that makes the
      first safe: one row, so a refusal happens before the INSERT and there is no half-written state
      to recover from. %>
  <% if @errors.present? %>
    <div id="reallocation-errors" class="rounded border border-status-danger bg-status-danger-light px-6 py-4">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-status-danger">Nothing moved</p>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-700">
        The ledger is exactly as it was. Fix the line below and try again.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-0.5 pl-5 text-sm text-status-danger">
        <% @errors.each do |error| %>
          <li><%= error %></li>
        <% end %>
      </ul>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# ONE FORM, TWO DESTINATIONS, exactly as the waterfall does it. The GET recomputes the damage
      against the ledger — `required`, `allocated_balances` and the category's own HoldingStatus are
      server-side readers, and a JavaScript copy of the schedule maths would be a second answer free
      to disagree with the one that writes. The POST submitter below carries the same fields, so what
      is written is what was shown. %>
  <%= form_with url: new_allocation_path, method: :get do %>
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded p-6 space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Where it's going</h3>

      <%# BOTH FIELDS SHARE ONE MEASURE (design review H10). The select ran the full width of the
          card while the amount box was 160px, so the two questions on one card read as two
          different KINDS of control — and at 1440px the select's caret sat 700px from its own
          label. `max-w-sm` on each wrapper is the smallest change that makes them one set: same
          left edge, same right edge, same width at every viewport, and neither of them as wide as
          the page. %>
      <div class="max-w-sm">
        <%# EXPLICIT IDS, not the param names, and this was measured on screen rather than reasoned
            about. `shared/_date_selector` — the month scrubber in the sidebar chrome — re-emits
            every scalar query parameter as `hidden_field_tag key, value` in EACH of its two forms,
            so a field whose id is the bare param name lands on a page holding three elements with
            that id. `label for=` and `getElementById` both reach the hidden one first:
            `document.getElementById("amount")` on this page returned
            `<input type="hidden" name="amount" value="300">`, not the box the user types in. %>
        <%= label_tag "move-to-category", "Envelope", class: "form-label" %>
        <%# FLAT, with Available first. The pool era grouped these by account and gave orphans a
            group of their own; there is one root now (spec §2), so there is nothing to group by —
            and Available is a real end of a real move, since it is where a sweep sends a closed
            category's leftover and where a savings withdrawal goes (§3). %>
        <%= select_tag :to_category_id,
                       options_for_select(
                         allocation_destination_options(@presenter.destinations),
                         @presenter.to_category&.id
                       ),
                       include_blank: "Choose an envelope",
                       id: "move-to-category",
                       class: "form-select" %>
      </div>

      <div class="max-w-sm">
        <%= label_tag "move-amount", "How much", class: "form-label" %>
        <%# `min` refuses a zero or a negative before it is submitted. Neither is an event and
            `Allocation` refuses both anyway — this only keeps the user from having to be told so. %>
        <%= number_field_tag(
              :amount,
              @presenter.amount_value,
              id: "move-amount",
              step: "0.01",
              min: "0.01",
              placeholder: "0.00",
              class: "form-input w-full text-right tabular-nums"
            ) %>
      </div>

      <%# WHAT THE MOVE BUYS. Only once both ends of it are known — before then there is no arrival
          to describe, and a line printed over an empty form would be describing nothing. %>
      <% if @presenter.gain %>
        <p id="reallocation-gain" class="text-sm text-gray-700">
          <%= allocation_gain_sentence(@presenter.gain) %>
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <% if @presenter.to_category %>
      <section id="reallocation-sources" class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded"
               aria-labelledby="sources-heading">
        <div class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-200">
          <h3 id="sources-heading" class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Where it's from</h3>
          <%# THE SOURCE IS NAMED IN A SENTENCE, NOT ONLY BY A RADIO (design review H10). This
              screen stated its destination in words at the top and left its OTHER end to a dot in
              a list of up to a dozen rows — and the one path that matters most arrives with a
              source already chosen: Home's "Take $120 from Groceries" button preselects it, so the
              user lands on a screen that has decided half the move for them and says so nowhere.
              Both ends of the move are now readable without hunting for a checked control.

              `sources.find(&:selected?)` is the presenter's own answer to "which row is ticked",
              the same Candidate the row below renders, so the sentence cannot name a source the
              list disagrees with. %>
          <% chosen = @presenter.sources.find(&:selected?) %>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-700" data-move-source>
            <% if chosen %>
              Taking it from <strong class="font-semibold"><%= chosen.name %></strong>.
            <% else %>
              No source chosen yet — pick one below.
            <% end %>
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600">
            Any envelope, and your unallocated money — nothing here moves between banks, so nothing
            is out of reach.
          </p>
        </div>

        <% if @presenter.sources.empty? %>
          <p class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-gray-600">
            There's nothing else holding money yet, so there's nothing to move from.
          </p>
        <% end %>

        <% @presenter.sources.each do |candidate| %>
          <%# SHOWN BUT DISABLED, WITH THE REASON. A source that cannot afford the move is rendered
              exactly like one that can — same row, same status, same figure — and differs only in
              that its control is dead and the line beneath it says why. A row that simply vanished
              would leave the user hunting for an envelope they can see on Home. %>
          <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 <%= "bg-gray-50" unless candidate.affordable? %>"
               data-source="<%= candidate.name %>">
            <% radio_id = "from-#{candidate.id}" %>
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
              <span class="flex items-baseline gap-2">
                <%= radio_button_tag(
                      :from_category_id,
                      candidate.id,
                      candidate.selected?,
                      id: radio_id,
                      disabled: !candidate.affordable?,
                      class: "form-checkbox"
                    ) %>
                <%= label_tag(
                      radio_id,
                      candidate.name,
                      class: allocation_source_label_class(candidate)
                    ) %>
              </span>
              <%# The category's own status, in the app's one row vocabulary (spec §4.4) — the same
                  words Home prints, so the user is choosing between rows they already know.
                  AVAILABLE has none: it holds money for no rule, so there is no state to report. %>
              <% if candidate.status %>
                <span class="text-sm text-gray-600">
                  <%= pool_status_label(candidate.status, period_closed: candidate.period_closed) %>
                </span>
              <% end %>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs <%= candidate.affordable? ? "text-gray-500" : "text-status-warning" %>"
               data-source-reason="<%= candidate.name %>">
              <% if candidate.affordable? %>
                <%= allocation_holding_clause(candidate) %>
              <% else %>
                Can't make this move — <%= allocation_reason(candidate) %>
              <% end %>
            </p>

            <%# THE DAMAGE, on every source that could actually make the move, so the choice between
                them is a comparison rather than a guess.

                Three colours for three different things, and the middle one is the whole point of
                the screen: grey when the move only takes money no rule was holding, amber when it
                reaches past that into a rule's reserve, red when it pushes the category into a
                different state altogether. %>
            <% if candidate.damage %>
              <p class="mt-1 text-xs <%= allocation_damage_class(candidate) %>"
                 data-damage="<%= candidate.name %>">
                ↳ <%= allocation_damage_sentence(candidate) %>
              </p>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </section>
    <% end %>

    <%# "UPDATE FIGURES" IS FIRST IN DOM ORDER AND `flex-row-reverse` PUTS IT BACK ON THE RIGHT.
        HTML's implicit submission activates THE FIRST SUBMIT BUTTON IN TREE ORDER when Enter is
        pressed in a number field — so with the move button first, typing an amount and pressing
        Enter would MOVE THE MONEY, on a screen whose whole purpose is stating the cost before that
        happens. The distribution screen shipped exactly that defect and it wrote a $2,900 split on a
        keypress. Enter recomputes; the example "recomputes rather than moving money when Enter is
        pressed in the amount box" is what holds it. Do not reorder these two without moving the
        reverse with them.

        The token rides on the button rather than in a hidden field, for _confirm's reason: this form
        is a GET, and a hidden `authenticity_token` would be serialised into the query string on
        every recompute — into the URL bar, the history and the logs. %>
    <%# STACKS BELOW `sm` (design review H6). Two 38px buttons and a sentence cannot share a 375px
        row: the pair wrapped under the helper text and the confirm ended up flush against the
        card's edge. The buttons take their own row, still in reverse DOM order so Enter reaches
        "Update figures" first — see above, and do not flatten this into one flex direction. %>
    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:justify-between gap-3 sm:gap-4">
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">Nothing moves until you press the button.</p>
      <span class="flex flex-row-reverse items-center gap-3">
        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-secondary">Update figures</button>
        <button type="submit"
                class="btn btn-primary"
                id="confirm-move"
                formaction="<%= allocations_path %>"
                formmethod="post"
                name="authenticity_token"
                value="<%= form_authenticity_token %>">
          Move the money
        </button>
      </span>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
